WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.627 --> 00:00:05.337
Galileo Galilei was the greatest astronomer
of all time.

00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:10.697
His discoveries in the 16th and 17th centuries
shocked the establishment, including the Catholic

00:00:10.697 --> 00:00:15.316
Church which, at the time, believed the earth
was the center of the universe.

00:00:15.316 --> 00:00:21.357
Armed with his telescope and a brilliant mind,
Galileo proved them wrong: the earth actually

00:00:21.357 --> 00:00:23.187
revolves around the sun.

00:00:23.187 --> 00:00:28.156
And for this assertion, he would be condemned
to a tragic life.

00:00:28.156 --> 00:00:33.587
A century before, a Polish scientist named
Nicolaus Copernicus had come up with the unorthodox

00:00:33.587 --> 00:00:37.296
idea that the Sun was at the center of the
solar system, not the Earth.

00:00:37.296 --> 00:00:39.736
Called the heliocentric model.

00:00:39.736 --> 00:00:42.507
Copernicus's theory was just that - a theory.

00:00:42.507 --> 00:00:44.857
Galileo proved Copernicus was right.

00:00:44.857 --> 00:00:49.686
He challenged many preconceived notions through
a rigorous process of testing.

00:00:49.686 --> 00:00:55.306
His reliance on scientific experimentation
may have started with a fascination with pendulums.

00:00:55.306 --> 00:01:00.407
As a student, he was struck by the steadiness
of a swinging lamp in the Cathedral of Pisa.

00:01:00.407 --> 00:01:04.597
He noticed that it didn’t matter how far
the chandelier swung, it always took the same

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:07.307
amount of time to complete one swing.

00:01:07.307 --> 00:01:11.557
Galileo used his own pulse to measure time
as there were no wristwatches back then.

00:01:11.557 --> 00:01:16.157
His experiments with pendulums confirmed that
it took exactly the same time for a pendulum

00:01:16.157 --> 00:01:19.607
to go back and forth, no matter the distance
it swung.

00:01:19.607 --> 00:01:23.887
His discovery led to the later invention of
the pendulum clock which some say opened the

00:01:23.887 --> 00:01:27.737
door for the Industrial Revolution in the
18th century as the development of timekeeping

00:01:27.737 --> 00:01:30.077
allowed for a faster pace of life.

00:01:30.077 --> 00:01:32.537
Galileo’s discoveries brought him some fame.

00:01:32.537 --> 00:01:37.107
He gained a teaching position at the University
of Pisa - the same university where he dropped

00:01:37.107 --> 00:01:38.457
out a few years earlier.

00:01:38.457 --> 00:01:42.457
His father insisted he study medicine though
he was more interested in math.

00:01:42.457 --> 00:01:44.197
He never graduated.

00:01:44.197 --> 00:01:49.007
While teaching at the university, academics
argued over Aristotle’s belief that heavier

00:01:49.007 --> 00:01:51.717
objects fell faster than lighter ones.

00:01:51.717 --> 00:01:54.227
Most scientists agreed with the ancient philosopher.

00:01:54.227 --> 00:01:55.327
Galileo did not.

00:01:55.327 --> 00:02:00.497
He reportedly set up a demonstration at the
leaning Tower of Pisa to prove Aristotle and

00:02:00.497 --> 00:02:01.517
his colleagues wrong.

00:02:01.517 --> 00:02:06.967
If a heavier object falls faster than a lighter
one as Aristotle maintained, it would look

00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:08.597
something like this.

00:02:08.597 --> 00:02:15.077
But Galileo showed that all objects – regardless
of their mass – freefall with the same acceleration.

00:02:15.077 --> 00:02:19.257
He was right but his smug nature was too much
for his colleagues who felt Galileo got a

00:02:19.257 --> 00:02:21.627
kick out of proving everyone wrong / humiliating
them for believing Aristotle.

00:02:21.627 --> 00:02:25.117
His contract at the University of Pisa was
not renewed.

00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:29.177
Fortunately, he found another teaching position
at the University of Padua

00:02:29.177 --> 00:02:33.036
He needed the work as his father had died
and he was entrusted with the care of his

00:02:33.036 --> 00:02:36.807
younger brother Michelangelo Galilei, who
became a composer.

00:02:36.807 --> 00:02:41.427
During his nearly two-decade tenure at the
University of Padua, his fame increased.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:47.286
In 1600, he met Marina Gamba, a Venetian woman
with whom he had three children out of wedlock,

00:02:47.286 --> 00:02:49.067
a son and two daughters.

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:53.227
Worried that his daughters wouldn’t marry
well, he urged them to become nuns in a convent

00:02:53.227 --> 00:02:58.477
near Florence despite the fact that he had
run into trouble with the Catholic Church.

00:02:58.477 --> 00:03:02.597
Galileo had challenged the church’s official
position that the Earth was the center of

00:03:02.597 --> 00:03:03.807
the universe.

00:03:03.807 --> 00:03:08.676
It all began in 1609 when he learned of a
simple telescope built by a Dutch spectacle

00:03:08.676 --> 00:03:09.676
maker.

00:03:09.676 --> 00:03:13.457
It was a mysterious invention that could make
distant objects appear closer.

00:03:13.457 --> 00:03:15.777
He soon built telescopes of his own.

00:03:15.777 --> 00:03:19.536
Some Venetian merchants saw their value as
a navigational tool and were eager to get

00:03:19.536 --> 00:03:20.707
their hands on them.

00:03:20.707 --> 00:03:25.316
While they did eventually find their way to
the decks of sailing ships, Galileo’s ambitions

00:03:25.316 --> 00:03:29.066
extended beyond the seas to the skies.

00:03:29.066 --> 00:03:32.267
He used his telescope to make startling discoveries.

00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:36.737
He found that the moon was not a perfect sphere
as Aristotle believed all heavenly bodies

00:03:36.737 --> 00:03:40.137
were but instead, was full of imperfections.

00:03:40.137 --> 00:03:45.377
These are Galileo’s own sketches showing
the moon’s valleys, deep chasms, and mountains.

00:03:45.377 --> 00:03:50.607
He discovered four moons orbiting Jupiter
- the largest planet in our solar system - destroying

00:03:50.607 --> 00:03:53.547
the notion that the heavens revolved around
Earth.

00:03:53.547 --> 00:03:57.257
His belief that the Earth wasn’t at the
center of the solar system was solidified

00:03:57.257 --> 00:04:01.487
when he discovered that Venus went through
phases, just like our moon.

00:04:01.487 --> 00:04:06.176
The nature of these phases could only be explained
by Venus going around the Sun, rather than

00:04:06.176 --> 00:04:07.176
the Earth.

00:04:07.176 --> 00:04:11.667
He concluded that Venus must travel around
the Sun, at times passing behind and beyond

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:12.667
it.

00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:16.867
Therefore, Earth was not the center of the
universe - the sun was.

00:04:16.867 --> 00:04:20.147
This angered the powerful people in academia
and the church.

00:04:20.147 --> 00:04:24.227
The majority of scientists and theologians
agreed with Aristotle's view that the Sun, other planets,

00:04:24.227 --> 00:04:28.316
and stars revolved around Earth.

00:04:28.316 --> 00:04:32.106
Galileo believed in God and was in awe of
the role that science played in the beauty

00:04:32.106 --> 00:04:33.636
of God’s creation.

00:04:33.636 --> 00:04:37.517
He wrote a letter to a student explaining
that his observations did NOT contradict the

00:04:37.517 --> 00:04:41.466
Bible because scripture was written from an
earthly perspective.

00:04:41.466 --> 00:04:45.427
But it nevertheless got him into trouble with
the Roman Catholic Church which, at that time,

00:04:45.427 --> 00:04:47.276
essentially ruled Italy.

00:04:47.276 --> 00:04:52.216
In 1633, he faced an Inquisition whose purpose
was to prosecute those considered to be enemies

00:04:52.216 --> 00:04:53.487
of the state.

00:04:53.487 --> 00:04:56.287
He was interrogated and threatened with torture.

00:04:56.287 --> 00:05:01.346
He was then charged with being “vehemently
suspect of heresy” and agreed to plead guilty

00:05:01.346 --> 00:05:06.367
and was forced to recant his scientific discovery
in exchange for a lighter sentence.

00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:10.337
His punishment was house arrest for the rest
of his life.

00:05:10.337 --> 00:05:14.556
Authorities banned his book championing his
belief in a sun-centered universe.

00:05:14.556 --> 00:05:19.246
And he had to stop teaching or otherwise spreading
his ‘heresy’.

00:05:19.246 --> 00:05:24.356
Galileo spent nine years under house arrest
at his villa in Arcetri near Florence before

00:05:24.356 --> 00:05:30.526
dying on January 8, 1642, at the age of 77.

00:05:30.526 --> 00:05:35.416
It took more than 350 years for the Vatican
to formally acknowledge its mistake.

00:05:35.416 --> 00:05:42.726
In 1992, Pope John Paul II apologized and
officially declared that Galileo was right.

00:05:42.726 --> 00:05:47.797
Galileo’s work influenced scientists for
generations to come, including Sir Isaac Newton

00:05:47.797 --> 00:05:49.216
who developed the law of gravity.

00:05:49.216 --> 00:05:52.976
I did a story about Newton which I’ll link
in my description.

00:05:52.976 --> 00:05:58.267
Today, astronomers refer to Jupiter’s four
moons as the Galilean satellites in honor

00:05:58.267 --> 00:05:59.267
of him

00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:03.506
In the centuries that followed Galileo’s
telescopic observations, telescopes have grown

00:06:03.506 --> 00:06:10.186
in size and power, revealing secrets of the
universe that even he could never have imagined.

